+### The `rx1` result — and a prediction that failed
+
+Once the model runs, both grids report their stiffness. From the driver's spin-up log:
+
+```bash
+grep -i stiffness ~/seaforward/forecast/model-runs/Agulhas_AGRIF/20260717_1way/spinup/croco_spinup.out
+```
+```
+ Maximum grid stiffness ratios:   rx0 = 0.2001   rx1 = 14.836     <- parent
+ Maximum grid stiffness ratios:   rx0 = 0.2115   rx1 = 13.416     <- child
+```
+
+**The child's `rx1` is *lower* than its parent's.** 13.42 against 14.84.
+
+That contradicts the prediction in A13. The reasoning there — "3× refinement resolves
+the same slope with thinner layers, so `rx1` gets worse" — sounded right and is not
+what happened.
+
+Compare the São Tomé child (Phase 8), built at the **same `rfact = 0.2` as its parent**:
+
+| | parent `rx1` | child `rx1` | child `rfact` |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| IGOG / São Tomé | 14.82 | **18.39** (worse) | 0.2 (same as parent) |
+| Agulhas | 14.84 | **13.42** (better) | **0.15** (lower) |
+
+So the reasoning does hold when `rfact` is held constant — São Tomé's child got worse
+exactly as predicted. The Agulhas child is better **because of the `rfact = 0.15`**,
+which more than compensated.
+
+**What this is honestly worth:** the caution was right in *effect*, but for reasons that
+weren't tested. The counterfactual — an Agulhas child at `rfact = 0.2` — was never run.
+It might have landed near 18 and been perfectly fine, since São Tomé's child sits at
+18.39 and is the best-behaved grid in this whole documentation set (Phase 8: RMSE 0.0042
+against Mercator, better than its own parent).
+
+The useful, defensible statements:
+
+- `rfact` on the child **does** control its `rx1`, and lowering it works.
+- A high `rx1` is **not** by itself a problem — 18.39 ran beautifully.
+- So treat `rx1` as **something to watch, not something to fix pre-emptively.** Build
+  with the parent's `rfact`, read the number, and only reach for a lower one if the
+  child actually misbehaves. Changing it up front means you never learn whether you
+  needed to.
+
